Block 2: The Bridge

    Python:

        API_URL = "https://your-render-app-name.onrender.com/chat"

What it is: This is the exact GPS coordinate of the backend brain you just deployed. Whenever the frontend needs to think,
            it will package data and mail it to this address.

Block 3: The Painter's Notebook (Session State)

    Because Streamlit forgets everything every time the screen refreshes, we have to give it a permanent notebook
    to write things down in. In Streamlit, this notebook is called st.session_state. Anything saved in here survives the screen wipes.

    Python:

        if "session_id" not in st.session_state:
            st.session_state.session_id = str(uuid.uuid4())

    What it does: Remember your backend FastAPI code? Pydantic demands a user_id so it can find the right file folder in active_sessions.

    The Logic: We tell Streamlit, "Look in your notebook. Do you have a session ID? If not, use UUID to generate a random
               string (like '8f3a-4b21...') and save it." Now, every time this specific user sends a message, they send
               this exact ID, so the backend knows who they are.

    Python:

        if "messages" not in st.session_state:
            st.session_state.messages = []

    What it does: We check the notebook for a chat history. If it's empty, we create a blank list [].
                  This list will hold a record of every message sent and received, so the painter knows what to draw.

Block 4: Drawing the History

    Python:

        for msg in st.session_state.messages:
            with st.chat_message(msg["role"]):
                st.markdown(msg["content"])

    What it does: Remember, Streamlit wipes the screen constantly. If you have a 10-message conversation,
                  how does it stay on screen?

    The Logic: Streamlit loops through the messages notebook. For every message, it opens a visual chat bubble
               (st.chat_message) and writes the text inside it (st.markdown). It repaints the entire history
               in a fraction of a millisecond.

Block 5: The Action Trigger

    Python:

        if prompt := st.chat_input("Ask the Swarm a question..."):

    The Walrus Operator (:=): This is a special Python trick. It does two things at once: It creates the visual text
                              box at the bottom of the screen (st.chat_input), AND it waits for the user to press Enter.
                              When the user presses Enter, it takes their text and assigns it to the variable prompt,
                              then triggers the if statement.

    Python:

        st.session_state.messages.append({"role": "user", "content": prompt})
        with st.chat_message("user"):
            st.markdown(prompt)

    What it does: First, we save the user's new message into the permanent notebook so it survives the next screen wipe.
                  Second, we immediately draw a user chat bubble on the screen with their text.

    Python:

        with st.chat_message("assistant"):
            with st.spinner("The Swarm is thinking..."):

    What it does: We draw an empty AI chat bubble on the screen, and put a spinning loading wheel inside it.

    Python:

        payload = {
            "user_id": st.session_state.session_id,
            "prompt": prompt
        }
        response = requests.post(API_URL, json=payload)

    What it does: This is the climax. We pack the cardboard box exactly how Pydantic demanded: the random user_id
                  from the notebook, and the prompt. We use requests.post to fire that box over the internet to your
                  Render server. The script pauses here and waits for Render to reply.

    Python:

        if response.status_code == 200:
            data = response.json()
            answer = data["final_answer"]
            routing = data["manager_routing"]

            st.markdown(answer)
            st.caption(f"🛣️ *System routed via: {routing}*")

            st.session_state.messages.append({"role": "assistant", "content": answer})

    What it does: If the server replies successfully (200 OK), we rip open the SwarmResponse package. We extract
                  the final_answer and the manager_routing string. We write the answer in the chat bubble, draw the cool
                  routing badge below it, and finally—most importantly—we save the AI's answer into the notebook so
                  the painter doesn't forget it on the next turn.